a smaller host long double fits in a larger target slot.

WHAT STOPS WITHOUT THIS. mc-tcc builds an x86_64 cross compiler, and that
compiler cannot emit a long double constant at all:

    lib/libtcc1.c:624: error: can't cross compile long double constants

x86_64-tcc IS A PROGRAM WHOSE OWN long double IS EIGHT BYTES, because mc-tcc
compiled it and micro-c maps float, double and long double to one word-sized
type -- patch 0001 sets LDOUBLE_SIZE 8 for exactly that reason. But it TARGETS
x86_64, where x86_64-gen.c declares LDOUBLE_SIZE 16. init_putv compares the two
and finds neither branch matches:

    sizeof(long double) == LDOUBLE_SIZE      8 == 16   no
    sizeof(double)      == LDOUBLE_SIZE      8 == 16   no
    memcmp(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, LDOUBLE_SIZE)   reads 16 bytes from an 8-byte field

THE TWO EXISTING BRANCHES ARE BOTH EQUALITY TESTS, and that is the whole bug.
The second one already does the right thing -- narrow the value and store it --
but only fires when the target slot is exactly the size of a double. A host
whose long double is SMALLER than the target's has a correct answer available
and no branch that reaches it.

WHY NOT SET LDOUBLE_SIZE 8 IN x86_64-gen.c, WHICH IS WHAT 0001 DID FOR arm64.
Because the two situations are not the same. Patch 0001 changes the size of a
type in a compiler that will only ever build the next compiler, and its
preamble is careful to say what that costs: "Code generated by this tcc gives
long double eight bytes, where the aarch64 Linux ABI gives it sixteen." Here
the same change would be made in a compiler that BUILDS musl, and that musl is
kept -- it is the libc the whole x86_64 ladder links against. musl's own
headers would then say LDBL_MANT_DIG 113 while every long double in it occupied
eight bytes, which is the exact mismatch rung 2 of rungs.sh spends thirty lines
diagnosing on the other architecture. A wrong ABI in a throwaway compiler is a
declared limitation; a wrong ABI in a retained libc is a defect.

SO THE TARGET TYPE IS LEFT AT SIXTEEN BYTES and only the host-side copy is
fixed. The generated code, the struct layouts and the calling convention are
all still x86_64's.

WHAT IS LOST, PRECISELY. A long double CONSTANT is stored with the eight bytes
the host compiler actually holds, so it carries double precision rather than
the eighty bits an x86 long double can represent. Nothing else narrows: this is
the initializer path only, and only for constants the compiler folds at compile
time. musl and gcc 4.7.4 do not depend on compile-time long-double constants
being exact beyond double, and the compiler that has this limitation is
discarded once gcc exists.

THE UPPER BYTES ARE ZERO, NOT GARBAGE, and that is a property of tcc rather
than an assumption. section_realloc zeroes every byte it adds:

    data = tcc_realloc(sec->data, size);
    memset(data + sec->data_allocated, 0, size - sec->data_allocated);
                                                        tccelf.c:284

so the eight bytes this does not write were already zero. An 80-bit x87 value
whose high bytes are zero is a denormal-or-zero rather than a random pattern,
which is the honest representation of "we only had a double".

THE CONDITION IS `<`, NOT `<=`. The equality case is already handled by the
first branch above, and writing it twice would mean two code paths for one
situation.

NOT UPSTREAM, AND THE UPSTREAM DISCUSSION IS WORTH KNOWING. Christian Jullien
reported the same guard firing on macOS/x86_64 in July 2021 -- "both double and
long double are 8 bytes but LDOUBLE_SIZE == 16" -- and the fix attempted there,
setting TCC_USING_DOUBLE_FOR_LDOUBLE for that target, was withdrawn as a
mistake. That define selects double-based branches in the float parser and does
NOT override the backend's LDOUBLE_SIZE, which is why passing it here changed
nothing: run 85537036642 probed for an eight-byte long double, correctly found
sixteen, declined the flag, and failed anyway.

MEASURED: with this, x86_64-tcc compiles lib/libtcc1.c.
--- a/tccgen.c
+++ b/tccgen.c
@@ -7898,6 +7898,8 @@
                     memcpy(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, LDOUBLE_SIZE);
                 else if (sizeof(double) == LDOUBLE_SIZE)
                     *(double*)ptr = (double)vtop->c.ld;
+                else if (sizeof(long double) < LDOUBLE_SIZE)
+                    memcpy(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, sizeof(long double));
                 else if (0 == memcmp(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, LDOUBLE_SIZE))
                     ; /* nothing to do for 0.0 */
 #ifndef TCC_CROSS_TEST
